  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Kuehneromyces lignicola, commonly known as the False Funeral Bell, is a small woodland fungus belonging to the Hymenogastraceae family. Despite its ominous common name, it is a relatively inconspicuous decomposer found throughout New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Cap appearance",
      "description": "Small, bell-shaped to convex cap, typically brown to reddish-brown in color, often with a distinctive darker center",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Gill structure",
      "description": "Gills are attached to the stem and densely packed, ranging from whitish to pale brown in color",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem characteristics",
      "description": "Thin, fragile stem that is typically lighter in color than the cap with a fibrous or grooved texture",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species grows on decaying hardwood logs, leaf litter, and woody debris in moist forests and wooded areas throughout New England, particularly in shaded understory environments.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The common name 'False Funeral Bell' likely refers to its somber bell-shaped appearance and dark coloring, though the actual origin of this evocative name remains unclear in mycological literature.",
